    pe: "GCS 12 (E3 M5 V4). Febrile (39.5°C), tachycardic, tachypnoeic. Mild jaundice. No neck stiffness. No focal neurology. Palpable spleen (3cm below costal margin). Petechial rash on lower limbs. BP 100/60.",
    vitals: "HR: 150 bpm, BP: 81/50 mmHg, RR: 36 breaths/min, Temp: 39.2°C, SpO2: 87%",
    labs: "Blood film: 12% parasitaemia (Plasmodium falciparum, mixed stages). Hb 8.5 g/dL. Platelets 35 x10^9/L. Glucose 52 mg/dL (hypoglycaemia). Creatinine 1.4 mg/dL. Bilirubin 3.5 mg/dL (unconjugated). LDH elevated. Lactate 4.5 mmol/L. Blood culture: drawn. HIV test: negative.",
    imaging: "CT head (if done): no acute pathology.",
    diagnosis: "Severe Falciparum Malaria with Cerebral Involvement, Severe Anaemia, Thrombocytopenia, and Hypoglycaemia (WHO Severe Malaria Criteria)",
    ddx: ["Cerebral Malaria", "Meningitis/Encephalitis", "Sepsis", "Febrile Seizure", "Cerebrovascular Accident"],
    goals: "Rapidly reduce parasitaemia, manage complications (hypoglycaemia, anaemia, seizures), prevent death",
    pharm: "1. First-line for severe malaria: IV Artesunate 2.4 mg/kg at 0, 12, and 24 hours, then daily (dose: 120mg IV for 50kg patient). 2. After minimum 24 hours of IV artesunate and when able to tolerate oral: complete treatment with oral Artemether/Lumefantrine (6-dose course over 3 days). 3. Supportive care: IV 10% Dextrose for hypoglycaemia (50mL bolus). Blood transfusion if Hb <7 g/dL or symptomatic. Monitor for seizures — give IV Diazepam 5-10mg if occurs. 4. Broad-spectrum IV antibiotics if septic (IV Ceftriaxone 2g daily) until cultures negative.",
    nonPharm: "ICU monitoring. Strict I/O. Cooling measures (Paracetamol, tepid sponging). Prevent bedsores. Maintain airway if GCS drops.",
    carePlan: "Admit to HDU/ICU. Start IV Artesunate immediately. Correct hypoglycaemia. Transfuse if Hb <7. Monitor: GCS q1h, parasitaemia q12h, glucose q4h, Hb, platelets, creatinine daily. Lumbar puncture if no improvement (to rule out meningitis).",
    dtps: "1. Life-threatening severe malaria with multi-organ involvement requiring urgent parenteral antimalarial therapy. 2. High mortality risk from cerebral malaria, severe anaemia, hypoglycaemia.",
    monitoring: "GCS, vitals hourly. Parasitaemia q12-24h. Glucose q4-6h. Hb, platelets, creatinine, bilirubin daily. Watch for: blackwater fever (haemoglobinuria), acute kidney injury, ARDS, bleeding (DIC).",
    counselling: "Malaria is a medical emergency when it affects the brain. IV Artesunate is highly effective — most patients start improving in 24-48 hours. Full recovery is possible with prompt treatment. Long-term: always sleep under an insecticide-treated mosquito net. Severe malaria can be prevented by early treatment of uncomplicated malaria.",
    followUp: "Repeat blood film daily until parasitaemia clears (<0.1%). If neurological sequelae, refer for rehabilitation. Review Hb in 2 weeks.",
    pearls: "IV Artesunate is superior to IV Quinine for severe malaria (reduces mortality by 22.5% in the AQUAMAT trial). Intramuscular Artesunate is an option if IV access is not available. Hypoglycaemia in severe malaria is common (from both disease and quinine historically, less with artesunate). Cerebral malaria has 10-20% mortality even with optimal treatment. Splenomegaly and thrombocytopenia are common. Do not use artemisinin monotherapy for uncomplicated malaria (requires ACT to prevent resistance).",
