    hpi: "John Akinyi, a 69-year-old Carpenter from Nyeri, presents with a 2-month history of chronic watery diarrhoea, 8 kg weight loss, persistent dry cough, low-grade fevers, night sweats, and oral thrush. Reports being sexually active with multiple partners. Never tested for HIV. Has had two sexually transmitted infections in the past 2 years (syphilis, gonorrhoea).",
    pmh: "Recurrent STIs (syphilis 2 years ago, gonorrhoea 1 year ago). No known TB contact.. Also has Type 2 Diabetes.",
    medHx: "No regular medications",
    allergies: "NKDA",
    pe: "Thin, wasted appearance (BMI 17). Oral thrush (white plaques on tongue and buccal mucosa). Mild cervical lymphadenopathy. Chest: clear. Abdomen: mild epigastric tenderness. No skin lesions. No hepatosplenomegaly.",
    vitals: "HR: 120 bpm, BP: 89/47 mmHg, RR: 37 breaths/min, Temp: 38.6°C, SpO2: 82%",
    labs: "HIV rapid test: Positive (Confirmatory ELISA positive). CD4 count: 68 cells/mm3 (severe immunosuppression). HIV viral load: 550,000 copies/mL. Hb 9.5 g/dL. WBC 3.2. Creatinine 0.8. CrAg (cryptococcal antigen): negative. Sputum Xpert MTB/RIF: Negative. CXR: normal. HBV serology: HBsAg negative. STI screening: syphilis RPR positive (1:8), treated.",
    imaging: "Chest X-ray: normal (no active TB).",
    diagnosis: "New Diagnosis of HIV-1 Infection with Severe Immunosuppression (WHO Stage 3: Oral Candidiasis, Unintentional Weight Loss, Chronic Diarrhoea)",
    ddx: ["HIV/AIDS", "Pulmonary TB", "Disseminated TB", "Lymphoma", "Chronic GI Infection"],
    goals: "Start ART, prevent OIs, manage IRIS, screen for TB/Crypto, partner notification, prevent transmission",
    pharm: "1. Start ART: Tenofovir Disoproxil Fumarate 300mg daily + Lamivudine 300mg daily + Dolutegravir 50mg daily (TLD regimen — preferred first-line in Kenya). 2. OI Prophylaxis: Start CTX (Co-trimoxazole) 960mg daily (for CD4 <200, continues until CD4 >200 for 6 months). 3. TB Preventive Therapy (TPT): Isoniazid 300mg daily + Pyridoxine 25mg daily for 6 months (after ruling out active TB). 4. If CD4 <100: Cryptococcal antigen screening. 5. Screen for STIs and treat if positive. 6. Immunisations: PCV13, PPSV23, annual influenza. Assess for hepatitis B and treat if co-infected.",
    nonPharm: "Nutritional support: ready-to-use therapeutic foods if malnourished. Safe water and food hygiene for diarrhoea. Barrier contraception (condoms) for prevention of transmission. Partner notification and testing. Family planning counselling.",
    carePlan: "Start TLD regimen (FDC = fixed dose combination, one tablet daily). Start CTX prophylaxis. Start INH/TPT. Schedule visit in 2 weeks for adherence check and side effects. Viral load monitoring in 6 months. Partner notification and testing. Refer to HIV support group.",
    dtps: "1. Advanced HIV with severe immunosuppression requiring urgent ART initiation. 2. High risk of OIs (PCP, TB, Cryptococcus) requiring prophylaxis. 3. Public health: transmission risk requiring prevention counselling.",
    monitoring: "CD4 count at baseline (not needed to start ART). Viral load at 6 months, then annually. Creatinine at baseline, 3 months (tenofovir renal safety). Hb (AZT effect). Weight and nutritional status. Adherence at every visit. Screen for TB symptoms at every visit.",
    counselling: "HIV is a manageable chronic condition. With one tablet daily, you can achieve an undetectable viral load, which means you cannot transmit HIV to others (U=U). Take your medication at the same time every day. Join a support group to connect with others living with HIV. Use condoms to protect your partners. You can live a long, healthy life.",
    followUp: "HIV clinic in 2 weeks. Viral load in 6 months (target <50 copies/mL). CD4 monitoring 6-monthly until >200. Annual cervical cancer screening (women). Annual STI screening. Long-term: CV risk, bone density (tenofovir), renal function monitoring.",
    pearls: "Kenya uses the \"Test and Treat\" strategy — start ART on the same day as diagnosis regardless of CD4 count. Dolutegravir is the preferred third agent in Kenya (TLD regimen) — potent, well-tolerated, high barrier to resistance. Tenofovir can cause renal impairment — monitor creatinine. CTX prophylaxis is life-saving at CD4 <200 (prevents PCP, toxoplasmosis, bacterial infections). U=U (Undetectable = Untransmittable) is scientifically proven when viral load <200 copies/mL.",
    references: ["Kenya Guidelines: HIV Prevention and Treatment", "WHO Guidelines: HIV/AIDS Management", "CDC: HIV Clinical Guidelines"],
