    pharm: "1. First-line empiric antibiotic: Nitrofurantoin 100mg BID for 5 days (preferred — low resistance, minimal GI effects, safe in early pregnancy). 2. Alternatives: Fosfomycin Trometamol 3g single dose sachet (good for MDR organisms). Pivmecillinam 400mg TID for 3 days. 3. If Nitrofurantoin unavailable or contraindicated: consider Oral Cefixime 400mg daily for 3-5 days. 4. AVOID: Amoxicillin, Co-trimoxazole, Ciprofloxacin (high resistance rates in Kenya for E. coli). 5. Symptomatic relief: Paracetamol for discomfort. Phenazopyridine 200mg TID for 2 days for dysuria (urine turns orange). 6. Encourage increased fluid intake.",
    nonPharm: "Increase water intake (2-3L/day). Empty bladder completely. Urinate after intercourse. Avoid holding urine. Wipe front to back.",
    carePlan: "Prescribe Nitrofurantoin 100mg BID for 5 days. Urine culture not required unless: pregnant, recurrent UTI, failed treatment, or complicated. If symptoms persist after 48 hours, send urine culture and consider pyelonephritis.",
    dtps: "1. Uncomplicated bacterial cystitis requiring short-course narrow-spectrum antibiotic. 2. Recurrence prevention requires behavioural modification.",
    monitoring: "Symptom diary: dysuria, frequency, urgency. If fever develops or flank pain, reassess for pyelonephritis. Post-treatment urine culture only if symptoms persist.",
    counselling: "Complete the full antibiotic course even if symptoms improve (usually 1-2 days). Drink plenty of water. Urinate when you feel the urge — do not hold it. Urinate soon after intercourse. Avoid douches or feminine hygiene sprays.",
    followUp: "Follow-up only if symptoms persist beyond 48 hours on antibiotics. If >2 UTIs in 6 months or >3 in 12 months, consider prophylactic antibiotics or vaginal oestrogen (postmenopausal).",
    pearls: "Uncomplicated UTI: affects otherwise healthy non-pregnant women with no anatomic/functional abnormalities. Nitrofurantoin is now first-line in most guidelines due to low resistance rates and minimal ecological impact. E. coli causes 75-90% of uncomplicated UTIs. In Kenya, E. coli resistance to Amoxicillin is >70%, Co-trimoxazole >60%, Ciprofloxacin >30% — these should not be used empirically. Pyuria without bacteriuria suggests urethritis (chlamydia/gonorrhoea) or interstitial cystitis.",
    references: ["IDSA Guidelines: Uncomplicated UTI", "Kenya Clinical Guidelines: UTI Management", "WHO: Antibiotic Stewardship in UTI"],
