    pearls: "Typhoid fever is a notifiable disease in Kenya. The classic \"stepladder\" fever pattern (gradually rising over days) is less common; most present with sustained high fever. Relative bradycardia (pulse-temperature dissociation) is a classic sign. Widal test is unreliable in endemic areas (high baseline titres) — culture confirmation is gold standard. Intestinal perforation typically occurs in the 2nd-3rd week of illness and presents with acute abdomen. Multi-drug resistant (MDR) S. Typhi and XDR (extensively drug-resistant) strains are emerging — Azithromycin and Ceftriaxone are current first-line options.",
